var e={kind:"band"},t={kind:"trace",range:"talk",since:"after"},a={kind:"flowtime",clock:"days",lines:["hvpg","varix"],title:"HVPG and varix over ten months"};var r={drugs:{propranolol:!1,carvedilol:!0},nsbbGain:1.3},s={edges:["C1a","C1b"],label:"Banded varices",kind:"treat"},o={id:"next-bleed",level:"core",title:"Preventing the next variceal bleed",minutes:8,objectives:["Explain why varices recur after endoscopic variceal ligation (EVL) alone","Combine carvedilol with EVL after a first variceal bleed","Judge the response to a nonselective beta-blocker (NSBB) from the fall in HVPG","Name when a TIPS follows failed secondary prophylaxis"],checks:[["Four EVL sessions eradicated the varices, yet a year later they have recurred. Why?","EVL obliterates the varix but leaves the portal pressure behind it unchanged, so new varices form as the bands slough. Endoscopy 3 to 6 months after eradication, then every 6 to 12 months, finds them and bands them again."],["What is first-line secondary prophylaxis after a first esophageal variceal bleed?","An NSBB with EVL, carvedilol preferred over propranolol (Baveno VIII 4.8)."],["On carvedilol the HVPG falls from 18 to 17 mmHg. Is that a response?","No: 6% is <10%, and 17 mmHg is still >12. A fall of \u226510%, or to \u226412 mmHg, counts (Baveno VIII Table 3)."],["He rebleeds despite carvedilol and EVL. What comes next?","A covered TIPS, with rifaximin considered beforehand to lower the risk of hepatic encephalopathy."]],summary:"After a first variceal bleed: why EVL alone lets varices recur, how carvedilol lowers the portal pressure behind them, why the two together are first-line, and the TIPS that follows when they fail.",slides:[{id:"after",terms:["varix"],preset:"cirr-decomp",action:e,cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:t,data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","hvpg"],key:["hvpg"],vignette:"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis and diuretic-controlled ascites had an esophageal variceal bleed 5 days ago, controlled with octreotide, ceftriaxone and EVL. He did not meet preemptive TIPS criteria.",kicker:"After the bleed",site:"sin",title:"The bleeding has stopped, the portal hypertension has not",line:"One band stopped the bleeding, but the varices are still {varix} and the HVPG {hvpg}. Without secondary prophylaxis, up to 60% rebleed within a year, most within 6 weeks.",notes:"Secondary prophylaxis starts before discharge, within 7 days of admission, because the rebleeding risk is highest in the first 6 weeks. The acute treatment did not lower the portal pressure: octreotide stops once the bleeding is controlled, and the band treats one varix, not the pressure behind it. He is Child\u2013Pugh B 8 and was not actively bleeding at endoscopy, so he did not meet the preemptive TIPS criteria (Child\u2013Pugh C 10 to 13, or B 8 to 9 with active bleeding). Patients who had a preemptive TIPS need no further prophylaxis; everyone else needs a plan before discharge. (AASLD 2024, prevention of rebleeding.)",ask:["Within how long of a variceal bleed should secondary prophylaxis start, and why?","Within 7 days of admission, because the rebleeding risk is highest in the first 6 weeks."]},{id:"gastric",terms:["varix"],cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"gastric",short:"Gastric bleed path",patient:{cp:8,fit:!0}},kicker:"After the bleed",site:"sin",title:"A fundal bleed would take another path",line:"Had the varix been fundal (GOV2 or IGV1), two phases would run: cyanoacrylate for hemostasis, then a TIPS as soon as possible, even at Child\u2013Pugh B 8.",notes:"His varices are esophageal, so he undergoes EVL and then takes carvedilol. A bleed from a GOV2 or an IGV1 varix is another event (Baveno VIII Fig. 3, 5.39 to 5.41): a high-risk bleed with two phases that start in parallel. The acute phase is cyanoacrylate injection or, in experienced centers, EUS-guided coils with cyanoacrylate. The definitive phase is a TIPS, with or without embolization of the varix, as soon as possible, for Child\u2013Pugh C <14 (so A and B too), age <75, creatinine <3 mg/dL and no heart failure. That is broader than the preemptive TIPS criteria for an esophageal bleed, which he did not meet at Child\u2013Pugh B 8 without active bleeding. When a TIPS is contraindicated, transvenous obliteration, endoscopic or surgical options follow after a team discussion. The Gastric varices talk follows this path.",ask:["Why does a Child\u2013Pugh B 8 patient get a TIPS after a fundal bleed but not after a controlled esophageal bleed without active bleeding?","A fundal (GOV2 or IGV1) bleed is high risk, and a TIPS is the preferred definitive treatment up to Child\u2013Pugh C 13. For an esophageal bleed the preemptive TIPS needs Child\u2013Pugh C 10 to 13, or B 8 to 9 with active bleeding."]},{id:"bands",terms:["varix"],action:[e,e,e],cam:"varices",mark:s,tool:t,data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","hvpg"],key:["varix"],delta:"after",kicker:"EVL alone",site:"sin",title:"EVL shrinks the varices, not the portal pressure",line:"EVL every 2 to 4 weeks shrinks the varices to {varix}. The portal pressure behind them does not fall: the HVPG is {hvpg}.",notes:"Each band ligates a variceal column, which necroses and sloughs over the next days. Sessions are repeated every 2 to 4 weeks (AASLD favors 4, to let post-banding ulcers heal) until the varices are eradicated. Obliterating the collateral removes a decompressing route for portal flow, so the portal pressure, if anything, rises slightly; the model shows such a rise, though the evidence for it in patients is weak. (AASLD 2024 statement 20.)",ask:["How often is EVL repeated after a variceal bleed, and until when?","Every 2 to 4 weeks until the varices are eradicated."]},{id:"slough",days:300,question:"Will the varices recur with EVL alone?",lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Varices banded",to:"10 months later"},terms:["varix"],cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:a,data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","hvpg"],key:["varix"],delta:"after",kicker:"EVL alone",site:"sin",title:"Bands slough and the varices recur",line:"Without surveillance for ten months, the varices regrow to {varix}: the portal pressure never fell. Hence endoscopy 3 to 6 months after eradication, then every 6 to 12 months.",notes:"EVL is a local treatment. As long as the portal pressure stays high, portal flow keeps reopening the left gastric (coronary) vein and the esophageal collaterals, and new varices form where the old ones were. Surveillance endoscopy finds them and bands them again, 3 to 6 months after eradication and then every 6 to 12 months (AASLD 2024). The slide shows the course with no endoscopy in between, to make the point. This is why EVL alone is not enough after a bleed. Post-banding ulcers form where the bands slough, and they can bleed. Baveno VIII calls this rare but serious (5.47). Management is individualized: a vasoactive drug, endoscopic hemostasis, a temporary stent or balloon tamponade, and a rescue TIPS when needed. The risk is highest with a MELD score >18 or after emergency banding. There is not enough evidence to give a PPI routinely to prevent these ulcers (5.48). Ulcers are one reason the sessions are spaced 2 to 4 weeks apart. The model loses one banding session's effect about every 50 days, a simplification of how varices return. (AASLD 2024 statement 20.)",ask:["Why do varices recur after eradication by EVL?","The portal pressure that caused them has not changed, so portal flow reopens the same collaterals."]},{id:"nsbb",preset:"cirr-decomp",params:r,action:e,cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:t,data:"tiles",tiles:["hvpg","hr"],hvpgResp:"after",key:["hvpg"],delta:"after",cite:"Baveno VIII 4.8, Table 3",kicker:"Carvedilol",site:"sin",title:"Carvedilol lowers the pressure behind the varices",line:"Once titrated, carvedilol lowers the HVPG from {hvpg@after} to {hvpg} and the heart rate to {hr}. A fall of \u226510%, or to {<=12 mmHg}, is a response.",notes:"Carvedilol starts at 6.25 mg/day and is increased to 12.5 mg/day if blood pressure allows. It reduces portal inflow, as propranolol does, and also lowers intrahepatic resistance, so it lowers the HVPG more; Baveno VIII prefers it after a bleed. A response is a fall in HVPG of \u226510% from baseline, or to \u226412 mmHg (Baveno VIII Table 3, which states it for primary prophylaxis; the guidelines cited here give no separate, stricter target after a bleed, so the same rule is applied). This man is a clear responder, with a fall of about 25%; many patients respond less. HVPG is rarely measured in practice, so the response is usually assumed. Reduce or stop carvedilol if the systolic blood pressure stays <90 mmHg, the MAP <65 mmHg, or AKI develops. (Baveno VIII 4.8, Table 3.) The response tile is the third tile on this slide because the fall is the point of it. A beta-blocker does not shrink the varices within days, so the varix is not shown here; in the model it follows the pressure at once.",ask:["What fall in HVPG marks a response to a beta-blocker?","A fall of \u226510% from baseline, or to \u226412 mmHg (Baveno VIII Table 3)."]},{id:"secprop",cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:{kind:"path",of:"secprop",short:"Next-bleed path",patient:{more:!0,can:"both"}},kicker:"Carvedilol",site:"sin",title:"His ascites adds TIPS and transplant to the options",line:"After a bleed, carvedilol with EVL comes first. His ascites is a second decompensating event, so Baveno VIII adds that TIPS and liver transplant may be considered.",notes:"This is the chart of Baveno VIII 4.8 to 4.10 for a patient recovering from an esophageal variceal bleed who is not eligible for rescue or preemptive TIPS, lit for the man on the slide. When the bleed is the only decompensating event, first-line prevention of rebleeding is an NSBB with EVL, carvedilol preferred over the other beta-blockers whether or not the patient took one before (4.8, strong). A patient who cannot receive one of the two continues the other alone (4.9, strong). A patient who also had a non-bleeding decompensating event, such as ascites or encephalopathy, may be considered for TIPS and liver transplant (4.10, a weak recommendation). He has ascites, so his path ends on that branch; the statement adds those options and does not withdraw carvedilol and EVL, which the next slides follow. He can take both treatments, which the chart takes from the case.",ask:["Which patients recovering from a variceal bleed, not eligible for a preemptive TIPS, may be considered for TIPS and transplant?","Those who also had a non-bleeding decompensating event, such as ascites or encephalopathy (Baveno VIII 4.10, a weak recommendation)."]},{id:"both",terms:["varix"],action:[e,e,e],cam:"varices",mark:s,tool:t,data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","hvpg"],key:["varix"],delta:"after",kicker:"Both together",site:"sin",title:"Carvedilol plus EVL is first-line",line:"EVL shrinks the varices to {varix}, and with carvedilol the HVPG is {hvpg}, against {hvpg@bands} with EVL alone.",notes:"Compared with EVL alone, adding an NSBB lowered rebleeding in every subgroup and improved survival in Child\u2013Pugh B and C. The two act on different targets: EVL obliterates the varix, and carvedilol lowers the portal pressure that refills it. A patient who cannot receive one of the two continues the other. (Baveno VIII 4.8; AASLD 2024 statement 38.)",ask:["Why combine an NSBB with EVL instead of EVL alone?","EVL obliterates the varix; the NSBB lowers the portal pressure that refills it. Together they reduce rebleeding further."]},{id:"held",days:300,lapse:{seconds:8,from:"Varices banded",to:"10 months later"},terms:["varix"],cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:a,data:"tiles",tiles:["varix","hvpg"],key:["varix"],delta:"after",kicker:"Both together",site:"sin",title:"With carvedilol the varices stay smaller",line:"Ten months later, the varices have regrown only to {varix}, against {varix@slough} with EVL alone, because carvedilol keeps the portal pressure lower. Surveillance endoscopy bands any recurrent varices.",notes:"The same 10 months as the EVL-alone slide, now on carvedilol. The bands still slough, but with a lower portal pressure, collateral flow is lower. The comparison is against the same time point with EVL alone.",ask:["After the same 10 months, why are the varices smaller on carvedilol than with EVL alone?","Carvedilol keeps the portal pressure lower, so less flow refills the collaterals once the bands slough."]},{id:"relation",cam:"varices",labels:["VAR"],tool:{kind:"curve",x:"hvpg",xCuts:[10],cuts:"ticks",patient:{preset:"healthy"},param:"cirrhosis",from:.3,to:1,step:.05,days:365,lines:["varix","wall"],dots:[{slide:"after",name:"After the bleed",on:[0],walk:"bands"},{slide:"bands",name:"Bands",on:[0],walk:"bands"},{slide:"slough",on:[0],walk:"bands"},{slide:"nsbb",name:"Carvedilol",on:[0],walk:"both"},{slide:"both",name:"Both",on:[0],walk:"both"},{slide:"held",on:[0],walk:"both"}]},kicker:"Summary",site:"sin",title:"EVL moves the varix down, carvedilol moves him left",line:"EVL shrinks the varix from {varix@after} to {varix@bands} without lowering the HVPG, and ten months later it is back at {varix@slough}. Carvedilol moves him left, to {hvpg@nsbb}.",notes:"The line is the model's cirrhosis from mild to severe, each point settled for a year, with the varix (left) and its wall stress as a fraction of the rupture stress (right) against the HVPG, as on the Varices talk. The dots are this talk's visits, in two walks. EVL: after the bleed the varix is 5.4 mm; three sessions take it to 3.2 mm, below the line, while the HVPG rises to 17.9 mmHg, because obliterating the collateral removes a decompressing route for portal flow. Ten months later the bands have sloughed and the varix is back on the line, at 7.2 mm. Carvedilol lowers the HVPG, so he moves left along the line, to 12.8 mmHg. With EVL as well his HVPG is 13.6 mmHg and the varix 2.6 mm; ten months later (the ring) the varix has regrown only to 5.0 mm, because the portal pressure is lower. EVL treats the varix and carvedilol treats the portal pressure. One model patient per point; real patients vary far more. (Baveno VIII 4.8; AASLD 2024 p. 1193.)",ask:["After EVL the varix is smaller and the HVPG has not fallen. Why does the varix recur?","The portal pressure that caused it persists, so portal flow reopens the same collaterals as the bands slough."]},{id:"summary",visual:"table",cols:["varix","hvpg","ppg"],asc:!1,rowHead:"Plan",of:[{id:"after",kicker:"Baseline",title:"After the bleed",ref:!0},{id:"bands",kicker:"EVL",title:"EVL alone"},{id:"slough",kicker:"EVL",title:"EVL, 10 months later"},{id:"nsbb",kicker:"Drug",title:"Carvedilol, one band"},{id:"both",kicker:"Both",title:"Carvedilol and EVL"},{id:"held",kicker:"Both",title:"Both, 10 months later"}],kicker:"Summary",title:"EVL treats the varix, carvedilol treats the portal pressure",line:"EVL obliterates the varices but leaves the portal pressure, so the varices recur. Carvedilol lowers the pressure and slows recurrence. Rebleeding despite both calls for a TIPS.",notes:"After a first esophageal variceal bleed: carvedilol (or propranolol) with EVL every 2 to 4 weeks until the varices are eradicated, then surveillance endoscopy. A TIPS follows rebleeding despite both, or comes earlier when another indication exists. A covered TIPS lowers the PPG to <12 mmHg and carvedilol stops, since AASLD 2024 does not require a beta-blocker after it; portal perfusion of the liver then falls sharply, so HE is the main risk, and rifaximin is considered beforehand. (Baveno VIII 4.13, 6.16; AASLD 2024 statement 39.) The bleed talk follows the TIPS itself. Fundal varices (GOV2, IGV1) follow another path: cyanoacrylate, BRTO or a TIPS (Gastric varices talk).",ask:["Which two treatments are first-line after a first variceal bleed?","Carvedilol or propranolol with EVL, repeated every 2 to 4 weeks until the varices are eradicated."]}]};export{o as a};
